<section class="nc-guide" id="guide">
  <div class="prose">
    <h2>Square feet and square meters for common spaces</h2>
    <p>1 square meter is 10.764 square feet. Note that this is not 3.28, the number of feet in a meter: area grows in 2 directions, so the length factor gets squared (3.2808 × 3.2808 = 10.764). Using 3.28 for an area is the single most common area-conversion mistake, and it understates the result by about 70%.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Space</th><th class="num">Square feet</th><th class="num">Square meters</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Small bedroom</td><td class="num">100</td><td class="num">9.29</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Primary bedroom</td><td class="num">150</td><td class="num">13.94</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1-bedroom apartment</td><td class="num">500</td><td class="num">46.45</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Small house</td><td class="num">1,000</td><td class="num">92.90</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Typical house</td><td class="num">2,000</td><td class="num">185.81</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Large house</td><td class="num">3,000</td><td class="num">278.71</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>For quick estimates, multiply square meters by 10, then add 7.5%: 50 m² → 500 + 37.5 = 537.5 sq ft (exact: 538.2). Going the other way, divide square feet by 10 and subtract 7%.</p>

    <h2>Lot sizes in acres</h2>
    <p>An acre is 43,560 sq ft. It has no fixed shape: a square acre is about 208.7 ft on each side, but a long, narrow acre is still an acre. For a sense of scale, an American football field between the goal lines (300 × 160 ft) is 48,000 sq ft, so an acre is about 91% of it.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Lot</th><th class="num">Square feet</th><th class="num">Acres</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Small city lot</td><td class="num">5,000</td><td class="num">0.115</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Suburban lot</td><td class="num">7,500</td><td class="num">0.172</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Quarter acre</td><td class="num">10,890</td><td class="num">0.25</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Half acre</td><td class="num">21,780</td><td class="num">0.50</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1 acre</td><td class="num">43,560</td><td class="num">1.00</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>A hectare is 10,000 m², or 2.471 acres. Land outside the U.S. is usually listed in hectares; 40 hectares is just under 99 acres.</p>

    <h2>Converting prices, not just areas</h2>
    <p>Price per unit area converts in the opposite direction from the area itself. A square meter is larger than a square foot, so the price per square meter is higher: $150 per sq ft is $1,614.59 per m². Multiply a per-square-foot price by 10.764 to get the per-square-meter price, and divide to go back. Flooring, rent, and construction quotes from different countries are only comparable after this step.</p>

    <h2>When square footage numbers disagree</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Listings measure differently.</strong> A home's advertised size may or may not include a finished basement, a converted garage, or an enclosed porch. Appraisers in the U.S. commonly follow the ANSI Z765 standard, which reports finished above-grade area separately from below-grade space.</li>
      <li><strong>Interior vs. exterior measurement.</strong> Measuring a house from its outside walls includes wall thickness, so it comes out larger than adding up the rooms inside.</li>
      <li><strong>Irregular rooms.</strong> Split an L-shaped room into 2 rectangles, find each area, and add them. Converting the total is more accurate than converting each wall length first and multiplying rounded numbers.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
